From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:49:56 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 115/119] fix: Sensor Clip lands inside the range the display
 can actually show

Round 3 test J2 reported that Sensor Clip 10 / 14 / 18 all look the same.
They do, and the diagnosis found why: the wiring is correct at every step,
but the ODT LUT's sampler does saturate() on its ACEScct input, and
ACEScctToLinear(1.0) = 222.86 = +10.30 stops over mid grey. Everything
brighter than that is one texel. Every clip value at or above 10.3 stops is
bit-identical on screen, so under the old 8..20 range most of the property
did nothing the user could see, and the default sat 3.7 stops inside the
dead zone.

'sclp' was introduced by 552318c6 (2026-08-29). It is ours, not a stock
CRYENGINE parameter, so its default is ours to change.

- Range<8,20,8,20> default 14 -> Range<4,12,4,12> default 8. Real cinema
  sensors hold 6-8 stops above 18 % grey (ARRI ALEV III ~7.4, Sony Venice
  ~7.5); the advertised "14+ stops" is the sensor's TOTAL range, most of it
  below grey. 8 is a real sensor and sits 2.3 stops inside the LUT domain,
  so it is visible.
- ToneMapping.cpp: the console clamp 4..24 becomes 4..16. 0.18 * 2^18.5 =
  65504 is the last finite half, so above 18.5 stops the shader's (half3)
  cast makes the level +Inf and the min() is silently dead - the clip reads
  as on and does nothing. The property cannot reach that, but the bus is
  writable from the console.
- Tooltip, README and ConsoleReference: drop the "14 matches the exposure
  meter's ceiling" rationale. It conflated two quantities that share a unit:
  kSceneReferredMeterCeilingStops is the METERING range, not a full well.
  They now say what the number means - stops over mid grey at which the
  sensor saturates, real sensors 6-8, above ~10 the display transform
  already shows white.
- SceneReferredLook.md gains a section on the LUT's domain being the
  pipeline's de-facto upper clip, and a troubleshooting row for it.

No shader change; the min() in HDRPostProcess.cfx was never the problem.
The clip's other consumer, the EXR export tap, is upstream of the ODT LUT
and carries the clipped value faithfully at any setting - a high clip is
invisible on the monitor, not meaningless in the negative.

